[vset RESOLV_VERSION 1.0.4]
[manpage_begin resolv n [vset RESOLV_VERSION]]
[see_also dns]
[see_also nslookup(1)]
[keywords DNS]
[keywords {domain name service}]
[keywords {name resolution}]
[keywords resolver]
[copyright {2002, Emmanuel Frecon}]
[moddesc   {Domain Name Service}]
[titledesc {Host name resolution with a cache}]
[category  Networking]
[require Tcl "8.5 9"]
[require resolv [opt [vset RESOLV_VERSION]]]
[description]
[para]

The [package resolv] package resolves host names to IPv4 addresses
using the [package dns] package. It adds two things to [package dns]:
it finds the name server configured on the local machine by itself,
and it keeps the results in a cache. The cache entries never expire,
which is usually good enough for short-lived applications.

[para]

The queries are sent to the name server found by [cmd ::resolv::init]
using the port, protocol and timeout set with [cmd ::dns::configure];
the [option -nameserver] setting of [package dns] is not used.

[section COMMANDS]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::resolv::init] [opt [arg defaultdns]] [opt [arg search]]]

Looks for the name server of the local machine, empties the cache and
returns the name or address of the name server that will be used, or
the empty string if there is none.

[para]

The name server is found by running [syscmd nslookup] on the name of
the local host. If this does not give a name server, [arg defaultdns]
is used. [arg search] is a list of domains: when a name to resolve does
not exist, it is tried again with each of these domains appended, in
order, until one of them exists.

[para]

[cmd ::resolv::resolve] calls this command without arguments if it has
not been called before. Call it with arguments to provide a fallback
name server or a search list, or call it again to empty the cache.

[call [cmd ::resolv::resolve] [arg hostname]]

Returns the IPv4 address of [arg hostname]. If the name has several
addresses, the first one is returned; if it exists but has no address,
the result is the empty string. A dotted IPv4 address is returned
unchanged without a query.

[para]

Results are taken from the cache if present, otherwise the name server
is queried and the result is added to the cache under [arg hostname].
The command waits for the answer, processing events meanwhile.

[para]

An error is thrown if there is no name server ([const {No dns server provided}]),
or if the query fails, for example if the name does not exist or the
server does not answer in time. The error message then starts with
[const {dns error:}] followed by the error message of [package dns].

[call [cmd ::resolv::ignore] [arg hostname]]

Removes [arg hostname] from the cache, so that the next
[cmd ::resolv::resolve] of it queries the name server again. It is not
an error if the name is not in the cache.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example {
package require resolv

# Use 192.0.2.53 if the local name server cannot be found, and look up
# names that do not exist in example.org as well.
resolv::init 192.0.2.53 example.org

puts [resolv::resolve www.example.org]
puts [resolv::resolve www]       ;# www.example.org, if www does not exist
puts [resolv::resolve 192.0.2.1] ;# 192.0.2.1, no query
}]

